      {
        "id": "thm-online-gradient-descent-regret",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Projected online gradient descent has static regret at most three-halves of DG square-root T",
        "deps": [
          "alg-online-gradient-descent",
          "def-static-dynamic-and-strongly-adaptive-regret",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-metric-topology",
          "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "def-convex-and-strictly-convex-functions-on-euclidean-sets",
          "def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient",
          "def-subgradient-and-subdifferential",
          "def-hilbert-space",
          "thm-euclidean-space-complete",
          "thm-projection-onto-a-nonempty-closed-convex-set",
          "lem-projection-onto-a-closed-convex-set-has-the-variational-inequality",
          "lem-metric-projection-onto-a-nonempty-closed-convex-set-is-nonexpansive",
          "thm-differentiable-convex-functions-and-gradient-inequality"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "dependency_level": 3,
      "statement": "Let $d,T\\ge1$ be integers, let $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $D,G\\ge0$ be finite bounds such that $\\|u-v\\|_2\\le D$ for all $u,v\\in K$ and $\\|g_t\\|_2\\le G$ for every round. Supply $x_1\\in K$. On each round, either $f_t$ is differentiable and convex on an open convex neighborhood of $K$ and $g_t=\\nabla f_t(x_t)$, or $f_t:C_t\\to\\mathbb R$ is convex on a convex set $C_t\\supseteq K$ and a vector $g_t\\in\\partial f_t(x_t)$ is supplied with $f_t(u)\\ge f_t(x_t)+\\langle g_t,u-x_t\\rangle$ explicitly required for every $u\\in K$; here $\\partial f_t(x_t)$ is the set of vectors satisfying that supporting inequality for every point of $C_t$. Run projected online gradient descent with $\\eta_t=D/(G\\sqrt t)$ when $D,G>0$, and with the no-op branch $x_{t+1}=x_t$ if $D=0$ or $G=0$. Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice only when $D,G>0$. For every realization satisfying these hypotheses and every $u\\in K$,\n$$\\sum_{t=1}^T\\bigl(f_t(x_t)-f_t(u)\\bigr)\\le\\frac32DG\\sqrt T.$$\nIf $D=0$ or $G=0$, this sum is nonpositive. Whenever the finite-horizon loss sum attains a finite minimum on $K$, the static regret, defined as $\\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(x_t)-\\min_{u\\in K}\\sum_{t=1}^T f_t(u)$, is therefore at most $\\frac32DG\\sqrt T$."
      }
    ]
